---
source: crates/tisty-cli/tests/mcp_catalogue.rs
expression: "rules(Some(\"ideas\"))"
---
## Brainstorm

An idea is a thought caught on the fly, before anyone knows whether it is something to do or something to keep: a few words, 140 at most, dated, on the Brainstorm sheet. It waits for the person to sort it out: it becomes a task, goes into the document on its subject, or is set aside.

Brainstorm is the person's sheet of ideas: an ordinary document with `brainstorm: on` in its properties, pinned at the top of the Library and shown as sticky notes, whatever title it carries in their language. `catch_up` names it and says how many ideas wait. Each idea is a sticky note, a `> [!IDEA]` callout headed by the minute it came and its colour — `> [!IDEA] 2026-11-12 09:40 · yellow` — with its words on the quoted lines under it, 140 characters at most. An idea is named in a call by the sheet's id, `#` and that heading: `46zdmrtt-0109#2026-11-12 09:40 · yellow`. `read_doc` brings the sheet by parts, oldest idea first, never as its outline; the window shows the newest first.

To jot ideas — the person's own, or ones you think up with them on a subject they asked about — `append_doc` on the sheet with one idea per paragraph, or one per item of a list. Tisty dates each one now and gives it its colour. An idea past 140 characters is refused: a longer thought belongs in a document. Never write a heading or a `[!IDEA]` line on the sheet, and never jot what the person did not ask for.

Sorting is the person's to ask for and theirs to decide. Read the sheet, then say in a few lines what you see: which ideas belong together and why, which already have a task or a document, which are old or done. Propose, then act on what they agree to:
- An idea that is something to do becomes a task: `propose` it in its list with `source` set to the idea's name, so it never makes two. Tisty takes it off the sheet, as the window's «Turn into a task» does.
- An idea that belongs in a document goes into the one on its subject — `find` it first — as the idea itself, added alone with `append_doc`: `> [!IDEA]`, the heading it has on the sheet and ` · right` on the first line, then its lines as the sheet has them, each opening with `> `. Tisty takes it off the sheet, as the window's «File in a document» does.
- A group of alike ideas, or one that grows into a plan, becomes a document or a passage of one, in your words, where its subject lives. Then mark each idea it gathered `moved`, with `instead` the document or task that holds it now.
- An idea that is done, out of date, a copy of another or a scratch is marked with `flag_doc`: its name in `doc`, several in one call, the `reason` — `duplicate_of` with `instead` the idea that stays, `moved`, `obsolete`, `wrong` or `scratch` — and in `body` why, in the person's language. The person sees a ◆ on each note, and deletes it or takes the mark off.

No other way takes an idea off the sheet: a write that leaves one out is refused, and so is marking the whole sheet, which only the person deletes or archives, from the window. Reword an idea only when the person asks, within 140 characters. Its place on the sheet and its colour are the person's.
